Queue<Anon> -- CS 452 HW1
Caleb Backer, September 2026

Files
  deq.h        given interface, unchanged
  deq.c        implementation
  error.h      given WARN/ERROR macros, unchanged
  main.c       test suite
  test.out     test output, the results of the run
  valgrind.out valgrind's report on the same run
  GNUmakefile  given makefile, which includes ../GNUmakefile

Building
This directory's makefile needs the course makefile in its parent
directory, as the skeleton does.  With it:

  make           build ./deq (deq.o and main.o linked together)
  make run       build and run the test suite
  make valgrind  run the test suite under valgrind
  make clean     remove built files

./deq exits 0 iff every test passes.  test.out and valgrind.out were
produced on onyx by "make run" and "make valgrind".

Design
A queue holds end pointers ht[Head] and ht[Tail] plus a length.  Each
node holds np[Head] and np[Tail], pointing toward the head and tail; a
missing neighbor is 0.  Indexing both arrays by the same End is what
makes the ends symmetric, so put, ith, get and rem are each written
once in terms of an end e and other(e).  deq_head_xxx() passes Head and
deq_tail_xxx() passes Tail.  unput(), which unlinks a node for both get
and rem, is itself a loop over the two ends.

The queue owns its nodes, not the data.  It never copies, inspects, or
frees a datum; deq_del()'s map function is how the caller frees its own
data.

Semantics
  put  append onto an end; len++
  get  remove from an end and return its datum, or 0 if empty; len--
  ith  return the datum i nodes in from an end, 0-based; len unchanged
  rem  remove the node nearest an end whose datum == d (pointers are
       compared, not contents) and return d, or 0 if absent; len-- iff
       found
  map  apply f to each datum, head to tail
  del  map f (if given) over the data, then free the queue
  str  return a freshly allocated, space-separated rendering; f is
       applied to each datum and its result freed, or the data are
       taken to be strings if f is 0

Errors
A zero queue or a failed malloc() is fatal: ERROR() reports it and
exits.  An out-of-range index is recoverable, so WARN() reports it and
0 is returned.  Absent data -- get on an empty queue, rem of a datum
the queue does not hold -- is a legitimate result, not an error, and
returns 0 quietly.

Testing
main.c runs 54 tests covering the empty queue, puts and gets at each
end, indexing from both ends including bad indices, rem from the middle
and from each end, rem of an absent datum and of one held twice, map,
str, and del over heap data.  Six warnings on stderr are expected: they
are the out-of-range tests, and test.out shows them where they occur.

Memory
Every node is freed by get, rem, or del, and every string from
deq_str() is freed by its caller.  valgrind reports no leaks and no
errors: 202 allocations, 202 frees.

Use of AI
I used Claude (Anthropic) as an assistant on this assignment: to review
my implementation against the assignment and rubric, to help word the
comments and this README, and to run the build, test suite, and
valgrind on onyx.  The design and the code are my own work, and I
understand and can explain all of it.
